steps.instructions:
`draw()` now calls `soundKeys()` every frame, and it is empty. In it, when the up arrow is pressed and `level` is below 10, add 1 to `level` and set the tune's volume from it again. When the down arrow is pressed and `level` is above 0, take 1 away and set the volume the same way:

```js
if (kb.presses('up') && level < 10) {
  level += 1;
  music.volume = level / 10;
}
```

and the same with `'down'`, `level > 0` and `level -= 1`. Use `kb.presses`, not `kb.pressing`: `presses` is true only on the frame the key goes down (6.3.3), so one press is one step, where `pressing` would step 60 times a second while the key is held. The `&& level < 10` stops the number at 10, where the tune is as loud as it gets. Run it, click the canvas, and press up and down: the words count between 0 and 10, and the tune follows.

`masterVolume(0)` silences every sound at once, the chime and the thud as well as the tune, without changing any sound's own volume. `masterVolume(1)` brings them all back, and `masterVolume()` with nothing in the brackets tells you the level it is at now (6.11.1). Under the volume keys in `soundKeys()`, make M flip it:

```js
if (kb.presses('m')) {
  if (masterVolume() > 0) masterVolume(0);
  else masterVolume(1);
}
```

Run it and press M: silence, and the words say `sound: off`. Press M again: everything comes back at the level it was.
